<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaʻeokulani</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 1794</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as Kaeokulani</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaʻeokulani was a Hawaiian ruler of the late 18th century, a brother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kahekili-ii">Kahekili II</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maui">Maui</a>. He became ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kauai">Kauaʻi</a> through marriage to its chiefess Kamakahelei, and also held Maui for a time. In 1794 he fought his nephew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kalanikupule">Kalanikūpule</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oahu">Oʻahu</a> and was defeated and killed after Kalanikūpule gained the help of the British trader William Brown. His son Kaʻumualiʻi succeeded him on Kauaʻi.</p></div>
